the Euro has created new opportunities  for citizens and businesses facilitating  cross-border trade and investment  bringing price stability expanding  business opportunities and making travel  cheaper but the crisis revealed that  closer coordination of economic policy  is needed to complement the euro to  realize its full potential to deliver  jobs growth social fairness and  financial stability closer integration  is needed in four areas and they all  depend on each other an economic union  in which all countries can grow more  prosperous together the financial union  so that banks insurers and financial  markets can better serve the needs of  European citizens and businesses a  fiscal Union to ensure stable and  sustainable public finances and a  political union to ensure Parliament's  have their safe Europe competes with the  rest of the world and our economies are  interdependent  how can we ensure that all countries  implement reforms so all our economies  become more competitive while ensuring  that social protection systems adapt to  new challenges and remain fair and  adequate households and businesses  across Europe need to be served by  strong banks and functional financial  markets how can we integrate our capital  markets increase funding opportunities  for companies and enhance confidence in  all banks regardless of their location  while making sure that the taxpayer does  not end up paying the bill in case of  poor management one country's tax and  spending policies affect the others in  the interest of the euro area as a whole  how can we make our fiscal policy  sustainable and responsive to economic  conditions and ensure everyone abides by  commonly agreed rules for healthy public  finances economic integration also  requires more collective decision-making  and a unified position on the wealth  stage how can we give parliaments our  biggest say and ensure that  internationally the countries of the  euro area speak with one voice a key  report by